Claim 19 — Repo chat / read-only question mode
==============================================

Goal: locate the user-facing entrypoint for repo chat / read-only
question mode; determine which channel surfaces it, whether it is
genuinely read-only, what it can inspect, and whether the worker
backend (Codex vs Claude Code) is operator-configurable.

Method: static inspection (Grep + Read) plus one safe runtime check
of the top-level CLI help.

Commands run
------------

1. MOLTBOT_STATE_DIR=$PWD/RELEASE_AUDIT/README_VERIFY/_state \
     node scripts/run-node.mjs --help
   exit 0.
   Subcommand list inspected end to end (head -40 + tail -50).
   Result: top-level subcommand inventory contains:
     setup, onboard, configure, config, doctor, dashboard, reset,
     uninstall, message, memory, agent, agents, acp, gateway, daemon,
     logs, system, models, approvals, nodes, devices, node, sandbox,
     gstack, tui, cron, dns, docs, hooks, webhooks, pairing, plugins,
     channels, directory, security, skills, update, status, health,
     sessions, browser, goal, help.
   No `chat`, `repo_chat`, `ask`, `question`, `inspect`, or similar
   read-only repo-chat subcommand is present on the CLI surface.

Code evidence
-------------

1. Telegram command spec — `/repo_chat` (and `/rc` alias) is the
   user-facing entrypoint.
   src/telegram/repo-chat-commands.ts:36-42

       export const REPO_CHAT_COMMAND_SPECS: Array<{ command: string; description: string }> = [
         { command: "repo_chat", description: "Ask a repository question (read-only)." },
         {
           command: "chat_backend",
           description: "Set repo chat backend: codex, claude_code, or off.",
         },
       ];

   Note: command description is explicitly "(read-only)".

2. Command registration (handler entry, with `rc` alias).
   src/telegram/repo-chat-commands.ts:401

       bot.command(["repo_chat", "rc"], async (ctx: TelegramRepoChatCommandContext) => {

3. Free-text routing path: in DMs/topics where a backend is configured,
   non-command + non-reply text routes to repo chat too.
   src/telegram/bot-handlers.ts:228-233

       export function shouldRouteTelegramTextToRepoChat(params: {
         repoChatBackend: "codex" | "claude_code" | null | undefined;
         replyToMessageId?: number;
       }): boolean {
         return isRepoChatBackendEnabled(params.repoChatBackend) && params.replyToMessageId == null;
       }

   Reply-routing also dispatches to repo chat when the reply targets a
   prior repo-chat message (src/telegram/bot-handlers.ts:458-475).

4. Worker entrypoint and read-only enforcement (Claude Code).
   src/repo-chat/repo-chat-worker.ts:8-17

       import {
         CLAUDE_ALLOWED_TOOLS_READ_ONLY,
         CLAUDE_READ_ONLY_PROMPT,
       } from "../goal/claude-code-constants.js";
       ...
       const CLAUDE_APPENDED_PROMPT = `${CLAUDE_READ_ONLY_PROMPT}\n\n${REPO_CHAT_CONTEXT}`;

   src/repo-chat/repo-chat-worker.ts:23-47

       export function buildClaudeRepoChatArgs(params: ... ): string[] {
         const args = [
           "-p",
           "--output-format", "json",
           "--verbose",
           "--allowedTools",
           CLAUDE_ALLOWED_TOOLS_READ_ONLY,
           "--append-system-prompt",
           CLAUDE_APPENDED_PROMPT,
         ];
         ...

   The Claude Code worker is launched with `--allowedTools` restricted
   to `CLAUDE_ALLOWED_TOOLS_READ_ONLY` and a system prompt that says
   "READ-ONLY. Do NOT create, modify, or delete any files."

5. The read-only tool allow-list (and the literal prompt string).
   src/goal/claude-code-constants.ts:1-4

       export const CLAUDE_ALLOWED_TOOLS_READ_ONLY = "Read,Glob,Grep,Bash";
       export const CLAUDE_ALLOWED_TOOLS_WRITE = "Read,Glob,Grep,Bash,Write";
       export const CLAUDE_READ_ONLY_PROMPT =
         "This is READ-ONLY. Do NOT create, modify, or delete any files.";

   IMPORTANT: `Bash` IS in the read-only allow-list (so the LLM can run
   shell commands like `cat`, `ls`, `git log`, etc.). `Write` is NOT in
   the list. Read-only-ness is enforced by (a) excluding the `Write`
   tool from `--allowedTools` and (b) a prompt instruction. There is no
   capability-level deny on, e.g., `bash -c "rm -rf …"` for the repo
   chat worker — the goal-system hard-deny scanner is wired into the
   goal worker tool surface (`src/goal/capability-enforcement.ts`),
   not into the repo-chat Claude Code subprocess. So the surface is
   "tool-restricted, not sandboxed" — Bash exposure is real and limited
   only by the LLM following the prompt and by the absence of `Write`.

6. Codex backend path (codex `exec`/`exec resume`).
   src/repo-chat/repo-chat-worker.ts:49-93

       export function buildCodexRepoChatArgs(params: { ... }): string[] {
         if (params.cliSessionId) {
           const args = ["exec", "resume", params.cliSessionId, "--json", "--skip-git-repo-check"];
           ...
         }
         const args = [
           ...(askForApprovalPlacement === "before_exec" ? ["--ask-for-approval", "never"] : []),
           "exec",
           ...(askForApprovalPlacement === "after_exec" ? ["--ask-for-approval", "never"] : []),
           "--json",
           "--color", "never",
           "--sandbox", "workspace-write",
           "--skip-git-repo-check",
           "--cd", params.workingDir,
         ];
         ...
       }

   The Codex form uses `--sandbox workspace-write`, NOT a read-only
   sandbox. The read-only-ness for Codex is enforced only by the
   prepended prompt instructions (`REPO_CHAT_CONTEXT` and
   `CODEX_STYLE_DIRECTIVE`). The prompt says "read-only" but the
   sandbox would technically allow writes inside the working
   directory.

7. Read-only system prompt and Moltbot project context shipped to both
   backends.
   src/repo-chat/repo-chat-context.ts:1-50

       // Repo-chat context for read-only code assistant sessions.
       ...
       "You are a read-only code assistant running inside the Moltbot
        gateway. Your job is to answer questions about the codebase
