    src/repo-chat/repo-chat-store.test.ts — session persistence.

What the worker can inspect
---------------------------

The repo chat worker is launched with `cwd = workingDir` (defaulting to
`path.resolve(process.cwd())` for fresh sessions:
src/telegram/repo-chat-commands.ts:327). So:

  * It can read everything under the gateway's working directory —
    typically the cloned source repo. With `Bash` in the allow-list, it
    can also run safe shell commands (`cat`, `git log`, `ls`).
  * It is NOT explicitly wired to inspect goal/run state under
    `~/.moltbot/goals/<runId>/`, worker outputs, attempt bundles, or
    journals. Nothing in `src/repo-chat/` references the goals state
    directory; the system prompt does not mention it either. A user
    *could* ask the bot to `cat ~/.moltbot/goals/...` and it might
    comply (subject to Bash file-system reach), but this is not a
    deliberate, documented surface.

Skipped checks
--------------

  * No live Telegram round trip was attempted (no bot token / chat
    available to this worker, and Telegram sends are out of scope for
    this read-only audit).
  * Claim that "repo chat is genuinely read-only" was NOT separately
    fuzz-tested; the verdict relies on the Claude Code
    `--allowedTools` list (Write excluded) and the prompt instruction.
    The Codex sandbox is `workspace-write`, so for Codex the read-only
    guarantee rests on prompt compliance, not on tool restriction.

Safe README wording (drawn from code, not aspirational)
-------------------------------------------------------

  * "Telegram-only: send `/repo_chat <question>` (alias `/rc`) to ask
    a question about the repo. Replies to the bot continue the same
    session."
  * "The backend is configurable to use Codex or Claude Code; switch
    with `/chat_backend codex` or `/chat_backend claude_code`."
  * "Repo chat is opt-in; until `/chat_backend` is set, the command is
    disabled."

Unsafe wording to avoid
-----------------------

  * "CLI command `moltbot chat …`" — no such CLI subcommand exists.
  * "Repo chat is sandboxed and cannot run shell commands" — the
    Claude Code allow-list explicitly includes `Bash`, and Codex
    runs with `--sandbox workspace-write`.
  * "Repo chat can introspect goal runs and worker artifacts under
    `~/.moltbot/goals/`" — no explicit code path wires this in; only
    the cwd-relative repo is the documented surface.
  * "Free / unlimited repo chat" — repo chat reuses the same Codex /
    Claude Code CLI subscriptions as the goal workers; no free tier
    is promised.

STATUS: partial
REASON: code path is real, well-factored, and unit-tested; backend
configurability is cited to a concrete code path
(src/config/types.telegram.ts:78 + src/telegram/repo-chat-commands.ts:475+).
Runtime presence on the CLI surface is absent (`moltbot --help` shows
no chat-like subcommand), so the live round-trip is Telegram-only and
manual-required. Read-only-ness is partial: Write is excluded from the
Claude Code allow-list but Bash is allowed and Codex runs with a
`workspace-write` sandbox; read-only-ness depends on prompt
compliance, not on capability enforcement.
